#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294} :activity/id #uuid "2f9b26dc-7c78-5bb7-9eb0-e6029679e710" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048455 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125737 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case.\n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$\n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit: $$\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eccfdda1-288a-5169-adde-a00163fa348f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2480 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a0c271d5-ca3c-5651-a78d-a2b18242c45c" :step/math-academy-id 11087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :question/id #uuid "a5753b43-b288-5cca-a26b-b060917182ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3330"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380054}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "9e271cad-793b-51f0-9124-47a943c42f3e" :step/math-academy-id 11090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :question/id #uuid "301263ef-3b68-54ac-87aa-5964484f5a14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3331"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049933}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380248}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "98229d4f-e216-5858-8b87-e9442dce8ede" :step/math-academy-id 11092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125738 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function of a single variable $f(x),$ if the left and right limits both exist and are equal at some point $x=a$, then this is sufficient to conclude that the limit exists at $x=a.$ \n\nSo, if we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x) = L,\n$$\n\nthen we may conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) = L.\n$$\n\nFor a multivariable function, the situation is not so straightforward. For a function of two variables $f(x,y),$ we may only conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f(x,y) = L,\n$$\n\nif we can show that the limit is the same *for all possible paths* on which $\\mathbf x$ can approach $\\mathbf{x}_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4b46a0a-5b1c-58eb-aa32-dd0ccd57f7a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2482 :tutorial/title "The Definition of a Limit: Single Variable Functions vs. Multivariable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a9b09430-2391-566d-ac41-6360e135665e" :step/math-academy-id 11094 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :question/id #uuid "56539c63-dd81-57c9-b7d2-18c935953203" :question/math-academy-id "e-3366"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380068}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "b6bdeefa-e28b-5e72-8c05-e9f8fb9c7542" :step/math-academy-id 11197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125760 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8f5c0d1f-e38c-5cd7-97e2-1aaaac5158b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2883 :tutorial/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "6285e0db-558c-5e11-85ab-0b82dc834963" :step/math-academy-id 15456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124373 :question/id #uuid "ae167d95-9cfd-5f31-91b1-484becd62677" :question/math-academy-id "e-6239"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050231}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345009}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "af7ce556-917b-54ac-a772-adecff1e36f2" :step/math-academy-id 23425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125815 :tutorial/content "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8040690e-6d4c-50ac-8b9f-f92615717816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3960 :tutorial/title "Proving That a Limit Does Not Exist by Evaluating It Along Different Paths"} :step/id #uuid "29991074-71d2-5a37-a971-5f658f37d3ba" :step/math-academy-id 23498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303}}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124730}]}
